<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seine</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Seine is a river of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, about 777 km long, which rises on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langres">Langres</a> plateau in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and flows through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troyes">Troyes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a> at Le Havre. Its basin was home to Gallic peoples such as the Parisii and Senones, and as the artery of Paris it made the region the political and economic heart of France. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> fleets used it to raid deep inland in the 9th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seine.jpg" alt="Seine" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mortimer62 · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bercy,_Paris_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Seine rises at Source-Seine, about 30 kilometres north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langres">Langres</a> plateau, and flows through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a> at Le Havre. Its drainage basin lies in the Paris Basin and covers most of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The river is divided into five parts: the Petite Seine from the sources to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montereau-fault-yonne">Montereau-Fault-Yonne</a>, the Haute Seine to Paris, the Paris waterway, the Basse Seine from Paris to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>, and the Seine maritime from Rouen to the sea. Ocean-going vessels can reach Rouen, 120 kilometres from the sea. Because the river is only 24 metres above sea level 446 kilometres from its mouth, it flows slowly and is easily navigated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name comes from the Gaulish Sēquana, the Celtic Gallo-Roman goddess of the river, to whom offerings were found at the source. The buried remains of a Gallo-Roman temple lie at the same site, and small statues of the goddess Sequana and other ex-votos found there are now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a> archaeological museum. The source has belonged to the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> since 1864.</p><p>On 28 or 29 March 845 an army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> led by a chieftain named Reginherus, possibly another name for Ragnar Lothbrok, sailed up the Seine with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-tower">siege towers</a> and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. On 25 November 885 another Viking expedition, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rollo">Rollo</a>, travelled up the river to attack Paris again. In March 1314 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a> had Jacques de Molay, last Grand Master of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a>, burned on a scaffold on an island in the river in front of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/notre-dame-de-paris">Notre Dame de Paris</a>. After the burning of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc">Joan of Arc</a> in 1431 her ashes were thrown into the Seine from the Mathilde Bridge at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>, though counter-claims persist without support.</p><p>On 9 August 1803 the American engineer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-fulton">Robert Fulton</a> made the first successful test of his steamboat on the Seine beside the Tuileries Garden. The Seine flooded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> severely in January 1910. In 1944 reaching the Seine was one of the original objectives of Operation Overlord, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> met it within 90 days of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normandy-landings">D-Day</a>. At the 1900 Summer Olympics the river hosted the rowing, swimming and water polo events.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Seine is 777 kilometres (483 miles) long, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> has 37 bridges across it, among them the Pont Alexandre III and the Pont Neuf. In 1991 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> added the banks of the Seine in Paris to its list of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Sites</a>. Four large storage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> built since 1950 on the Seine and its tributaries help to keep the river level constant through the city. The river inspired many artists of the 19th and 20th centuries, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-monet">Claude Monet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camille-pissarro">Camille Pissarro</a>, Georges Seurat and Alfred Sisley.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Seine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Trade existed but was constrained by poor infrastructure: rivers such as the Rhine, Meuse and <b>Seine</b> were the main commercial arteries, linking inland markets, episcopal cities and monastic fairs to North Sea trading centres such as Dorestad and Quentovic, where Frisian merchants dominated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Duchy of Normandy grew out of a Viking settlement on the lower <b>Seine</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From the Tagus estuary the first moves were by sea: south into the Mediterranean, with enclaves in south-western Spain, southern France and the Po Valley, and north to Brittany, from where routes along the Loire and <b>Seine</b> brought maritime beakers to the Lower Rhine about 2600 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/bell-beaker-culture">Bell Beaker culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>…as in the Grafenbühl tomb, red kermes dye, found at Hochdorf, and probably wine came north, and the finest imports include the Greek Vix krater, the Etruscan lebes from Sainte-Colombe-sur-<b>Seine</b>, the Greek hydria from Grächwil, the Greek cauldron from Hochdorf and the Greek or Etruscan cauldron from Lavau.</blockquote><a href="/culture/hallstatt-culture">Hallstatt culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>On the Continent the Vikings rowed up the <b>Seine</b>, the Loire and the Rhine.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>It is believed that before the arrival of Christianity in France a Gallo-Roman temple dedicated to Jupiter stood on the site of Notre-Dame, on the Île de la Cité, an island in the <b>Seine</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/notre-dame-de-paris">Notre-Dame de Paris · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
